# Target C-II - asymmetric THIN braid/fence extraction gives exact ordered uniqueness normal forms

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only geometric/combinatorial strengthening of the THIN growing-fence and phase-cone theorems; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** THIN side of the 15-spaced one-sign strong-TB1 Horn upper family through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** logarithmic THIN braid/fence count; inverse-`D` directional-capacity lemma; stopped-train step-cone monotonicity; proper-crosscut realization of complete trains; superdiameter thrackle theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_thin_asymmetric_unique_grid_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The preceding phase-cone theorem used four fixed train guards in order to retain a
`Theta(log D)` braid on `D^(1/2-o(1))` common rows. The growing-fence count has a
useful asymmetric regime which is stronger for incidence questions: keep
`Theta(log D)` braid chords but only `sqrt(log D)` trains on each flank. Synchronizing
the train fence then costs only `D^o(1)`, so the braid may use the much larger
`5^{-r}` budget.

This gives a common system with

\[
r_D=\left({1\over2\log5}+o(1)\right)\log D
\tag{AU.1}
\]

pairwise-crossing THIN chords,

\[
s_D=(1+o(1))\sqrt{\log D}
\tag{AU.2}
\]

fixed trains on each circumcenter flank, and

\[
\boxed{|A_D|=D^{1/2-o(1)}}
\tag{AU.3}
\]

common inverse-`D` phase rows.

After deleting only `O(1)` braid chords near the three phase-sector boundaries and
retaining one of the three direction sectors, one obtains one of two exact normal
forms.

1. **ROW-UNIQUE.** A `Theta(log D)` pairwise-crossing sub-braid meets every row in
   `A_D` exactly once. After retaining another `D^(1/2-o(1))` rows, the order of all
   braid intersections is identical on every retained row. A further
   Erdos--Szekeres extraction gives `Theta(sqrt(log D))` braid chords whose pairwise
   mutual crossings all lie on one side of the whole retained row slab.

2. **GUARD-UNIQUE.** A `Theta(log D)` pairwise-crossing sub-braid meets every one of
   the `2s_D` fixed fence trains at most once. Every such intersection is a proper
   crossing. Relative to the common fence order, the guards met by one chord form one
   consecutive interval. After keeping one orientation class these intervals are
   nested. Since a nested chain on `2s_D` guards has at most `2s_D+1` distinct sets,
   there is a sub-braid of size
   \[
   \boxed{
   \left({1\over24\log5}-o(1)\right)\sqrt{\log D}
   }
   \tag{AU.4}
   \]
   whose chords have exactly the same `0/1` intersection pattern against all
   `2s_D=(2+o(1))sqrt(log D)` fixed guards.

There is an additional topological strengthening which is useful in both cases. The
set of selected path rows met by any complete train is an interval in the path order.
Consequently every fixed fence train, not only every braid chord, crosses the entire
consecutive selected-row block from `min A_D` through `max A_D`. That block has length
at least `D^(1/2-o(1))`. The near-`pi/3` purification is still asserted only on the
rows of `A_D`; the interval extension is topological and does not promote the
intermediate crossings to the same quantitative angle estimate.

This removes two remaining ambiguities at once. The ROW-UNIQUE side now has a genuine
fixed braid order on a polynomial-height row subsystem. The GUARD-UNIQUE side no
longer carries an arbitrary binary braid/fence incidence matrix: it is a nested
interval system, and a balanced `sqrt(log D)` by `sqrt(log D)` exact homogeneous
subsystem survives.

---

## 1. Asymmetric use of the growing-fence count

Retain the notation

\[
q_0=0.05470272542658\ldots,
\qquad
T_*=3.0605,
\]

and let `N=Theta(D)` be the owned THIN blocker population. The finite
braid/fence count proved previously gives, uniformly for growing parameters in the
range used below, fixed objects on at least

\[
\left({3\over4}-o(1)\right)
{N\over 5^r}
\left({q_0\over T_*}\right)^{2s}
\tag{AU.5}
\]

common phase-pure rows. The same estimate follows directly from the greedy matching
product and the two flank falling-factorial counts.

Choose

\[
\boxed{
r_D=\left\lfloor{\log D\over2\log5}\right\rfloor,}
\qquad
\boxed{
s_D=\left\lfloor\sqrt{\log D}\right\rfloor.}
\tag{AU.6}
\]

The matching correction remains negligible because

\[
{r_D^2D^{3/4}\over D}\to0,
\tag{AU.7}
\]

and the train falling-factorial correction remains negligible because

\[
{s_D^2\over D}\to0.
\tag{AU.8}
\]

No convergence-rate assumption on the existing `o(1)` terms is needed for the
exponent statement: raising a factor `c-o(1)` to `O(log D)` contributes only
`D^{o(1)}`.

Now

\[
5^{r_D}=D^{1/2+o(1)}
\tag{AU.9}
\]

and

\[
\left({T_*\over q_0}\right)^{2s_D}
=\exp\!\left((8.04884009424\ldots+o(1))\sqrt{\log D}\right)
=D^{o(1)}.
\tag{AU.10}
\]

Since `N=Theta(D)`, (AU.5) yields (AU.3).

Thus there are fixed families

\[
F_D,\qquad L_D,\qquad R_D
\]

with

\[
|F_D|=r_D,
\qquad
|L_D|=|R_D|=s_D,
\tag{AU.11}
\]

such that

- the chords in `F_D` are vertex-disjoint THIN hull chords and every two cross
  properly;
- the `2s_D` trains in `L_D union R_D` are pairwise noncrossing and have one common
  order on every row of `A_D`;
- every chord and every fixed train crosses every row of `A_D`;
- all rows of `A_D` have one projective transverse phase to `O(D^-1)`;
- every left train crosses each retained row below `-0.01D`, and every right train
  crosses it above `+0.01D`.

---

## 2. A complete train crosses an interval of the selected path

Let

\[
\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m
\]

be the selected O--O path rows in their path order. They are pairwise disjoint proper
crosscuts of the outer-face disk, and every intermediate row separates the earlier
rows from the later rows.

### Lemma AU.1 - connected-crosscut interval support

For any path `gamma` in the disk, define

\[
J(\gamma):=\{j:\gamma\cap\tau_j\ne\varnothing\}.
\]

If `gamma` meets both `tau_i` and `tau_k` with `i<k`, then it meets every
`tau_j`, `i<j<k`. Hence `J(gamma)` is an integer interval whenever it is nonempty.

### Proof

Choose points

\[
x\in\gamma\cap\tau_i,
\qquad y\in\gamma\cap\tau_k.
\]

For `i<j<k`, the proper crosscut `tau_j` separates `tau_i` from `tau_k` in the
selected path disk. Any subpath of `gamma` joining `x` to `y` must cross `tau_j`.
This holds for every intermediate `j`. `square`

Apply the lemma to each fixed complete train `b in L_D union R_D`. Since `b` meets
every row in `A_D`,

\[
\boxed{
[\min A_D,\max A_D]\subset J(b).
}
\tag{AU.12}

The exact selected-cut support of every THIN braid chord is already an interval and
contains `A_D`, so the same inclusion holds for every `e in F_D`.

Put

\[
K_D=[\min A_D,\max A_D]\cap\mathbb Z.
\tag{AU.13}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{|K_D|\ge|A_D|=D^{1/2-o(1)},}
\tag{AU.14}
\]

and every braid chord and every fixed fence train intersects every selected row in
`K_D`.

Equation (AU.14) is only a topological grid statement. The phase and angular-defect
bounds inherited from the double count are asserted on `A_D`, not automatically on
all of `K_D`.

---

## 3. The three phase sectors applied to the asymmetric system

Use the common retained-row phase `[u_*]`. As in the phase-cone theorem, the row-step
sector and the two possible column-train step sectors divide the projective direction
circle into three open `pi/3` sectors

\[
\mathcal S_-,\qquad \mathcal S_0,\qquad \mathcal S_+,
\tag{AU.15}
\]

where `S_0` is the row-step sector and `S_-`,`S_+` are the two possible fixed-train
step sectors.

Delete every braid chord whose projective direction lies in an `L_0/D` neighborhood
of one of the three sector boundaries, with `L_0` larger than all fixed phase-error
constants. The inverse-`D` directional-capacity lemma, applied with the THIN shell
`D-1/(4D)<|e|<D`, deletes only `O(1)` chords.

One sector therefore contains a sub-braid `F_D^0` with

\[
\boxed{
|F_D^0|\ge{r_D-O(1)\over3}
=\left({1\over6\log5}-o(1)\right)\log D.
}
\tag{AU.16}

There are two cases.

### Case R - `F_D^0` lies in `S_-` or `S_+`

Every chord direction is outside the closed step cone of every retained row. The
line/train monotonicity lemma gives at most one intersection with a row, while exact
selected-cut support gives at least one. Thus

\[
\boxed{
|e\cap a|=1
\quad(e\in F_D^0,\ a\in A_D).
}
\tag{AU.17}

This is the ROW-UNIQUE branch.

### Case G - `F_D^0` lies in `S_0`

Every fixed fence train has one of the two column transverse phases relative to
`[u_*]`, so its step cone is a perturbation of `S_-` or `S_+`. The sector clearance
therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
|e\cap b|\le1
\quad(e\in F_D^0,\ b\in L_D\cup R_D).
}
\tag{AU.18}

Moreover the signed line-normal coordinate used in the line/train uniqueness proof is
**strictly monotone** along the oriented train. Hence whenever the intersection in
(AU.18) exists, the train passes from one side of the chord line to the other. It is
not a tangential one-point contact. Thus every nonempty chord/guard intersection in
Case G is a proper crossing.

---

## 4. ROW-UNIQUE has a polynomial-height constant braid order

Assume Case R and order the rows of `A_D` by their selected-path indices. Delete a row
if it contains the crossing point of two chords of `F_D^0`. The rows are pairwise
disjoint, so each of the

\[
{ |F_D^0|\choose2}=O((\log D)^2)
\]

chord-crossing points deletes at most one row. This is negligible compared with
`|A_D|=D^(1/2-o(1))`.

Every remaining row meets every braid chord exactly once. Orient the rows consistently
and record the permutation of braid intersections along the row.

### Lemma AU.2 - one chord pair changes row order at most once

For two straight braid chords `e,f`, their relative intersection order along the
ordered disjoint rows can change only across the unique point `e cap f`. Hence it can
change in at most one gap between consecutive retained rows.

### Proof

The two straight chords are simple arcs with exactly one proper mutual crossing. Cut
the disk along any retained row. Because both chords meet that row exactly once, the
relative order of their two row intersections records on which side of `e cap f` the
row lies. Two ordered disjoint rows with different relative orders must separate the
unique crossing point. Disjoint rows occur linearly along the selected path, so this
can happen in only one row gap. `square`

Across the whole braid there are at most

\[
{ |F_D^0|\choose2}
\]

row gaps at which the full intersection permutation changes. Therefore one
consecutive block in the `A_D` order has constant full braid permutation and size at
least

\[
{ |A_D|-O((\log D)^2)\over { |F_D^0|\choose2}+1}
=D^{1/2-o(1)}.
\tag{AU.19}

Call this row family `A_D^ord`. We have proved

\[
\boxed{
\text{every row in }A_D^{ord}\text{ sees the entire braid in one identical order.}
}
\tag{AU.20}

The fixed train fence already has one identical order on every retained row, so Case R
contains two simultaneous fixed orders on the same polynomial-height row subsystem.

There is a useful further extraction. Fix any row in `A_D^ord`. Order the braid
chords by their endpoints on one complementary hull shore of that row. Since every
two chords cross, the endpoint order on the other shore is reversed. The common row
intersection order is one permutation of these chords. Erdos--Szekeres gives a
monotone subsequence `G_D` with

\[
\boxed{
|G_D|\ge\sqrt{|F_D^0|}
=\left({1\over\sqrt{6\log5}}-o(1)\right)\sqrt{\log D}.
}
\tag{AU.21}

If the row order on `G_D` agrees with the first-shore endpoint order, the chord arcs on
that side of the row are pairwise noncrossing, so every mutual braid crossing lies on
the opposite side. If the row order is reversed, the symmetric statement holds.
Because the row permutation is the same throughout `A_D^ord`, the same side works for
the entire row slab. Thus

\[
\boxed{
\text{all pairwise crossings of }G_D
\text{ lie beyond one side of the }A_D^{ord}\text{ slab.}
}
\tag{AU.22}

Inside the slab the selected chords form an ordered noncrossing bundle, despite being a
pairwise-crossing hull braid globally.

---

## 5. GUARD-UNIQUE incidence is an interval, not an arbitrary bit word

Assume Case G. Fix one row `a_0 in A_D`. Its proper crosscut divides the outer
boundary into complementary arcs `P,Q`. Every fixed guard crosses `a_0`, so each guard
has one boundary endpoint on `P` and one on `Q`. Every braid chord also crosses
`a_0`, so its two hull endpoints likewise lie one on each arc.

Write

\[
G=L_D\cup R_D,
\qquad m:=|G|=2s_D.
\tag{AU.23}

The guards are pairwise disjoint proper crosscuts. Order their endpoints on `P` as

\[
p_1,\ldots,p_m
\]

and orient the order on `Q` compatibly, so their other endpoints occur as

\[
q_1,\ldots,q_m.
\tag{AU.24}

For a braid chord `e`, let `p(e) in P`, `q(e) in Q` be its endpoints and define

\[
\alpha(e)=\#\{j:p_j\text{ precedes }p(e)\},
\qquad
\beta(e)=\#\{j:q_j\text{ precedes }q(e)\}.
\tag{AU.25}

Thus `alpha,beta in {0,...,m}`.

### Lemma AU.3 - exact guard-incidence interval

For every `e in F_D^0`,

\[
\boxed{
\{j:e\cap g_j\ne\varnothing\}
=\{\min(\alpha,\beta)+1,\ldots,\max(\alpha,\beta)\}.
}
\tag{AU.26}

The set is empty when `alpha=beta`.

### Proof

A proper crosscut `g_j` separates the disk into two sides. In the compatible boundary
orders, `p(e)` and `q(e)` lie on opposite sides of `g_j` exactly when the Boolean
statements

\[
j\le\alpha(e),
\qquad
j\le\beta(e)
\]

differ. This is equivalent to `j` lying strictly between the two ranks in the sense
of the right side of (AU.26).

If the endpoints are on opposite sides, the chord must meet `g_j`. Conversely, by
(AU.18) there is at most one intersection, and the strict line-normal monotonicity from
Section 3 makes any such intersection a proper crossing. Therefore a meeting changes
sides and occurs exactly in the endpoint-separation case. `square`

Thus the exact `0/1` incidence word against all `m=Theta(sqrt(log D))` guards is one
contiguous interval. No Ramsey or arbitrary binary-pattern purification is needed.

---

## 6. Pairwise crossing makes the guard intervals nested after one split

Order the chords of `F_D^0` by their `P` endpoints. Their actual `P` endpoints are
distinct. Since every two braid chords cross properly, their `Q` endpoint order is the
reverse order in the compatible `Q` orientation.

Consequently

\[
\alpha_1\le\alpha_2\le\cdots,
\qquad
\beta_1\ge\beta_2\ge\cdots.
\tag{AU.27}

Split the chords into the two orientation classes

\[
\alpha_i\le\beta_i
\quad\text{and}\quad
\alpha_i>\beta_i,
\tag{AU.28}
\]

assigning equality to the first class. Retain the larger class. It has at least

\[
{|F_D^0|\over2}
\tag{AU.29}

chords.

In the first class, (AU.26) is

\[
I_i=\{\alpha_i+1,\ldots,\beta_i\},
\]

and (AU.27) makes the sets weakly decrease by inclusion. In the second class,

\[
I_i=\{\beta_i+1,\ldots,\alpha_i\},
\]

and the sets weakly increase by inclusion. Hence in either case

\[
\boxed{
\text{the exact guard-incidence sets form one nested chain.}
}
\tag{AU.30}

A nested chain of subsets of an `m`-element set has at most `m+1` distinct sets, since
every strict inclusion changes cardinality by at least one. Therefore some one exact
incidence interval occurs on at least

\[
{ |F_D^0|\over 2(m+1)}
\tag{AU.31}

chords. Using (AU.2), (AU.16), and `m=2s_D`, this is

\[
\boxed{
\left({1\over24\log5}-o(1)\right)\sqrt{\log D},
}
\tag{AU.32}

which proves (AU.4).

Every selected chord in this homogeneous sub-braid has exactly the same intersection
set against all `2s_D` fixed trains, and every `1` is exactly one proper crossing.
The common set is a consecutive interval in the global fence order and may be empty.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The THIN branch now admits an exact ordered reduction at a scale larger than the prior
four-guard or generic binary-incidence normal forms.

In ROW-UNIQUE, one retains

```text
Theta(log D) pairwise-crossing THIN chords;
D^(1/2-o(1)) rows with one exact common braid-intersection order;
2 sqrt(log D) fixed ordered fence trains;
and a Theta(sqrt(log D)) sub-braid whose mutual crossings all lie beyond one side
of the row slab.
```

In GUARD-UNIQUE, one retains

```text
Theta(log D) pairwise-crossing THIN chords with nested guard-incidence intervals;
2 sqrt(log D) fixed ordered train guards;
Theta(sqrt(log D)) chords with one identical exact interval incidence word;
D^(1/2-o(1)) common phase-pure rows;
and a consecutive D^(1/2-o(1))-long selected-row block crossed topologically by
all selected chords and all fixed guards.
```

The remaining THIN problem is therefore no longer intersection multiplicity or an
arbitrary braid/fence incidence matrix. In the ROW-UNIQUE branch the missing step is
metric: rule out a long near-diameter braid which traverses a polynomial-height
near-equilateral slab as one fixed noncrossing order and performs all mutual crossings
outside one end. In the GUARD-UNIQUE branch the missing step is likewise metric:
rule out a `sqrt(log D)` homogeneous almost-diameter braid whose exact crossings with a
`sqrt(log D)` noncrossing train fence form one common consecutive guard interval.

No contradiction with extremality is claimed here. C-II and Level I remain open.
